STEP 3 — HYPOTHESES
[HYP] GraphQL WAF bypass via form-encoded POST on app.n26.com/graphql
class: AUTH
asset: app.n26.com/graphql
confidence: 62
reasoning: POST application/json → 403/WAF-timeout; GET `?query=` → connection reset. Cookie set by app backend is literally `n26.graphql_form_payload`, implying server accepts form-encoded GraphQL payloads; WAF rule appears keyed to Content-Type+METHOD, so x-www-form-urlencoded or alternate method may reach the engine. `/api/graphql` shares the same 403 block.
evidence_needed: Any non-403/index response containing GraphQL errors, `__typename` echo, or introspection schema.
verify_steps: (1) POST https://app.n26.com/graphql with `Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, body `query={__typename}`, cookies from app.n26.com session, (2) same POST to /api/graphql, (3) PUT/PATCH https://app.n26.com/graphql with `Content-Type: application/json` body `{"query":"{__typename}"}`, (4) POST with `Content-Type: multipart/form-data` body `query=query{__typename}`
impact: GraphQL schema + mutation surfaces → banking ops, ATO. Severity: HIGH
testability: AUTH_HELPED
[HYP] Statsig server-side-only key path on flags.n26.com
class: MISCONFIG
asset: flags.n26.com
confidence: 48
reasoning: All /v1/* (initialize/get_configs/evaluate/log_event) return 403/401 regardless of key — consistent with server-secret-key RBAC, not a client-keyable instance. `client.*.js` 404 means either bundle path moved or integration is server-side only. app.n26.com shell (319KB) may host bundles under hashed paths; Statsig browser SDK requires a *publishable* client key, usually `client_<...>` in JS.
evidence_needed: Extract client key from real bundle; flips /v1/initialize 403→200/JSON.
verify_steps: (1) GET https://app.n26.com → parse all `src=`/`href=` of JS/CSS bundles and extract exact URLs, (2) GET each bundle URL, grep `statsig|client_key|client-`  (or sha256 after download), (3) GET https://flags.n26.com/v1/initialize?client_key=<extracted>
impact: If client-scoped, flag manipulation (limits/gates). Severity: MEDIUM
testability: PASSIVE
[HYP] spc.n26.com analytics pixel route reflection check
class: OTHER
asset: spc.n26.com
confidence: 40
reasoning: Regex route serves identical pixel bytes for any `v\d+/\w+`, no param reflection into body; `sp=` cookie + P3P = consent/tracking collector; ACAO `*` + Allow-Credentials mismatch is config noise only. Residual question: whether any variant of the pixel route (different methods, `_` segments, /api nesting) renders non-GIF content.
evidence_needed: Any route returning text/JSON/reflected input rather than the static GIF.
verify_steps: (1) GET https://spc.n26.com/api/v1/events, (2) GET https://spc.n26.com/v1/pixel/collect, (3) GET https://spc.n26.com/com.snowplowanalytics.snowplow/tp2 (Snowplow ingest probe)
impact: If collector, low (pixel only). Severity: LOW
testability: PASSIVE

STEP 4 — SELF-CRITIQUE
[PARKED] spc.n26.com payment BOLA/IDOR (prev 80-82): 200s are static 1x1 GIF on regex catch-all — bodies identical across /v1/{transactions,payments,tokens}, /api/v1, /v2/transactions; `image/gif`, no data/param reflection, longer paths 404. No confirmed financial API; prior "confirmed versioned endpoints" was body-blind status-code reading. Drops below threshold.
[PARKED] spc.n26.com analytics-collector hypothesis: pixel bytes static, HEAD/GET divergence explained (GET-only health route), no S&D-relevant impact; tracking/privacy not in scope for infra program. Confidence 40 → drop as no real logic flaw.
[FINAL] GraphQL WAF bypass via form-encoded POST (confidence: 62, rank: 1) — Survives: class AUTH in-scope, >40, concrete verify_steps, `n26.graphql_form_payload` cookie is direct evidence of server-side form-payload support.
[FINAL] Statsig SDK key extraction (confidence: 48, rank: 2) — Survives: class MISCONFIG in-scope, >40, passive verify_steps; slightly lowered because 403-on-initialize suggests server-only RBAC.

STEP 5 — NEXT ACTION
[NEXT] PROBE: POST https://app.n26.com/graphql with `Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, body `query={__typename}`, cookies `n26.csrf`, `num26UniqueDeviceToken`, `n26.graphql_form_payload` from a fresh app.n26.com session — record status/body; repeat against /api/graphql; read-only introspection only.

STEP 6 — LEARNING
[LEARN] REJECTED IDOR @ spc.n26.com: The /v1/transactions|payments|tokens 200s are a 1x1 GIF + `sp=` Snowplow cookie served by regex route `^(/api/)?v\d+/\w+$` — analytics collector, not payment API. Endpoint-map inference without body inspection produced a false positive.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ spc.n26.com: /health is GET-only (200 "OK" text/plain); HEAD 404 — earlier "rotated to 404" was an HTTP-method artifact. ACAO `*`/reflected-Origin + Allow-Credentials true on pixel route is config noise, not exploitable.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED AUTH @ app.n26.com: POST application/json → 403/WAF timeout; GET `?query=` → connection reset. `n26.graphql_form_payload` cookie strongly implies form-encoded GraphQL payloads are server-supported — untested bypass vector.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ flags.n26.com: All /v1/* 403/401 independent of client_key — pattern consistent with server-secret RBAC; client-key extraction only viable if a publishable key exists in app bundles.

STEP 7 — RISK
[RISK] n26: 25/100 — Private program (bugs.olivermaicher.eu), 1 rps ceiling, Envoy+WAF on GraphQL with connection-reset behavior, comprehensive nonce-CSP, Statsig RBAC solid so far. Top hypothesis (payment IDOR) disproven by body inspection; remaining value is GraphQL Content-Type bypass and possible Statsig key — both unconfirmed. No credit is claimed for non-deterministic findings; spc.n26.com reframed as tracking collector, low value.
